From: Daryl Denson <9751141+denson9874@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 15:13:40 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] husky: keep USB accessory mode in the kernel

CP3A's usb_accessory_utils sets ro.vendor.usb.userspace.aoa.enabled at
early boot from an aconfig flag, and init.zuma.usb.rc then turns on
ro.usb.userspace.aoa.enabled, which hands accessory mode (wired Android
Auto) to the userspace aoad daemon. This platform has no aoad (no AID, no
policy), so set the vendor property to false at build time: read-only
properties can't be set twice, and accessory mode stays on the kernel's
accessory function, as with the CP2A blobs.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 husky/vendor.prop | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/husky/vendor.prop b/husky/vendor.prop
index cd4c7b5..48173d9 100644
--- a/husky/vendor.prop
+++ b/husky/vendor.prop
@@ -44,3 +44,8 @@ persist.vendor.vibrator.hal.context.scale=60
 ro.vendor.vibrator.hal.device.mass=0.222
 ro.vendor.vibrator.hal.loc.coeff=2.8
 vendor.audio.hapticgenerator.distortion.output.gain=0.38
+
+# USB: AOA (accessory mode, wired Android Auto) through the kernel accessory function.
+# CP3A vendor can hand it to the userspace aoad daemon, which this platform lacks;
+# usb_accessory_utils sets this from an aconfig flag, and a build prop is read-only.
+ro.vendor.usb.userspace.aoa.enabled=false
